WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
The most important development work often happens before any screens are built.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.385
Start by defining the audience, their current level of knowledge,

00:00:07.385 --> 00:00:09.540
and what they are expected to do after the course.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.645
A useful course goal is not simply that learners understand a topic.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:15.130
It should describe a decision, action, behavior,

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.450
or judgment they need to perform more reliably.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.355
Separate what learners need to know from what learners need to do.

00:00:20.355 --> 00:00:22.905
Background information may be necessary, but it should support performance.

00:00:22.905 --> 00:00:26.180
For example, a learner may need to recognize when a process applies,

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.660
choose the correct step, avoid a common mistake, or explain a requirement to a customer.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.295
Those outcomes lead to better screen design and better assessment questions than a long

00:00:34.295 --> 00:00:35.690
list of facts.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.415
Planning also defines scope and constraints.

00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:41.225
Decide the course length, structure, interaction level, delivery format,

00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:44.640
accessibility expectations, completion rules, and assessment style.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.175
Some courses need a polished narrated experience.

00:00:47.175 --> 00:00:49.320
Others need a concise reference-like structure.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.615
Some must report completion to an LMS.

00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:54.420
Others may be used as informal enablement material.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.820
Storyboarding and scripting reduce rework.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:00.330
A storyboard does not need to be elaborate, but it should show module flow, key screens,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.955
narration, media ideas, interactions, and quiz alignment.

00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:06.570
Opening the authoring tool before the instructional plan is clear usually leads to

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:07.760
rearranging screens,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.155
rewriting narration, and rebuilding interactions later.

00:01:11.155 --> 00:01:13.660
Planning is how a course avoids becoming a decorated document.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.340
A strong planning document should be short enough for stakeholders to read but specific

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.900
enough to guide development.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.100
Include the audience, goal, module outline, assessment approach,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.380
accessibility expectations, review roles, and publishing target.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.350
That document becomes the reference point when someone asks to add unrelated content or

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.380
change the course direction late in the project.
